WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.524
<v SPEAKER_00>Hey, Travis Albritton here.

00:00:01.564 --> 00:00:11.505
<v SPEAKER_00>So I hope these episodes have been super helpful for you getting your show off the ground and having the confidence that you need to really launch a podcast, which is such an incredible thing.

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:30.881
<v SPEAKER_00>Now, I recently had the opportunity to sit down for a conversation with Eric Newsom, who has been an executive producer on some of the biggest podcasts in the world, many of NPR's podcasts, Ted's podcasts, and just brings a lot of insight and depth of wisdom to podcasting.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.088
<v SPEAKER_00>And so I wanted to share this with you as kind of like a podcasting 201.

00:00:35.607 --> 00:00:45.685
<v SPEAKER_00>This interview is what to do once you have your show going and you really want to see what's the next step, what's the next level that I can get to with my podcast.

00:00:45.965 --> 00:00:49.773
<v SPEAKER_00>He just wrote a brand new book on podcasting called Make Noise.

00:00:50.213 --> 00:00:52.579
<v SPEAKER_00>I will leave a link to the book in the episode description.

00:00:52.618 --> 00:00:53.820
<v SPEAKER_00>It's a fantastic book.

00:00:54.182 --> 00:00:58.210
<v SPEAKER_00>So I hope that this conversation is helpful for you and that you get a lot out of it.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.597
<v SPEAKER_00>And without further ado, here's my conversation with Eric Newsom.

00:01:05.471 --> 00:01:14.826
<v SPEAKER_01>So my name is Eric Newsom, and I spent most of the early part of my career in broadcast and eventually worked my way up to working at NPR.

00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:28.808
<v SPEAKER_01>And I started there in 2004, and less than a year later, I was in the cafeteria line at NPR, and the guy who was our COO at the time was behind me trying to make some kind of awkward chitchat.

00:01:29.349 --> 00:01:31.332
<v SPEAKER_01>He says, well, what's interesting that you've seen lately?

00:01:31.393 --> 00:01:33.295
<v SPEAKER_01>And I said, well, there's this podcasting thing.

00:01:33.334 --> 00:01:36.819
<v SPEAKER_01>And I started explaining to him in the lunch line, just to kind of make conversation.

00:01:36.859 --> 00:01:40.965
<v SPEAKER_01>He's like, oh, come by and give me a little spiel on it.

00:01:41.025 --> 00:01:44.049
<v SPEAKER_01>And so I came, I made an appointment, went and gave him a spiel.

00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:50.938
<v SPEAKER_01>A couple of weeks later, he shows back up at my door and says, you have a team of eight and you have 12 weeks.

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:54.265
<v SPEAKER_01>And at the end of that 12 weeks, we want there to be NPR podcasts.

00:01:54.965 --> 00:01:55.706
<v SPEAKER_01>I'm like, okay.

00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:57.269
<v SPEAKER_01>And we actually delivered it a month.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:58.070
<v SPEAKER_01>We got an extra month.

00:01:58.912 --> 00:01:59.492
<v SPEAKER_01>We delivered it.

00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:00.894
<v SPEAKER_01>It was 32 podcasts.

00:02:01.135 --> 00:02:06.745
<v SPEAKER_01>And then for the following decade, I kind of remained kind of the editorial lead on NPR podcasts, both podcasts.

00:02:06.745 --> 00:02:16.459
<v SPEAKER_01>figuring out how to take NPR programming and have it thrive in the podcast world, sound authentic there, and also making new things that were intended originally to be in that space.

00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:18.722
<v SPEAKER_01>And did that for a decade.

00:02:18.923 --> 00:02:32.382
<v SPEAKER_01>And then a couple of years ago, probably four and a half years or so ago, I left NPR and went to Audible, which is part of the Amazon extended universe, and created the original content team there.

00:02:32.802 --> 00:02:45.401
<v SPEAKER_01>And Then about a year or so ago, one of my friends and I left and started Magnificent Noise, which is a podcast production and consultation company based in New York.
